 Espace Client Entreprise is XXX's corporate customer portal for companies and entrepreneurs to manage all their XXX insurance contracts (Health & Protection, Retirement, Fleet, etc.)
  in one place. The Home page displays contract tiles that navigate to a Contract Listing page (with contract management and documents), an Employee page (to view, add, and remove employees on a contract), and
  an Account page showing the logged-in customer's account details.

ECP (Espace Client Particulier) is Allianz France's customer-facing web portal for individual (retail) customers, allowing them to view and manage their insurance contracts, documents, notifications, and
  personal data online.

CIAM (Customer Identity & Access Management) — Espace Client Entreprise is a security and identity overhaul for XXX's Corporate Customer Area (ECE Ulysse), introducing strong dual authentication via a
  group-approved CIAM provider. It extends access management so corporate customers can grant, monitor, and revoke third-party access (e.g., accountants, HR) to all or part of their portal and contracts. The
  scope covers customer, third-party, and assistance/agent journeys — including self-service account activation, password recovery, security notifications, and support workflows — across all corporate products
  and customers using ECE Ulysse.


 XXX Customer Portals (ECE, CIAM, ECP) — Worked across three interconnected XXX applications: Espace Client Entreprise, a 360° multi-domain portal for corporate customers to manage
  contracts, employees, and accounts; CIAM, a security initiative implementing strong dual authentication and third-party access management for the corporate portal; and ECP (Espace Client Particulier),
  Allianz France's retail customer portal for managing contracts, documents, and personal data. As Automation Architect, designed and built the test automation framework (Selenium, Playwright) integrated into
  CI/CD pipelines, ensuring quality and regression coverage across all three applications.

How did you architect scallable test automation framework?
During my tenure as Principal SDET & Automation Architect at Allianz, I led the design and development of a reusable test automation framework across four enterprise platforms. The challenge was to build a highly scalable and reliable framework that supports both UI and API automation for different business domains.
To address this, I architected a hybrid automation framework leveraging Java as the core programming language, combined with Selenium WebDriver for UI automation and REST Assured for API automation. I adopted the Page Object Model design pattern to promote separation of concerns, ease of maintenance, and reusability across the test suites.
The framework was designed with data-driven principles, allowing test cases to be easily parameterized and extended. For BDD-style tests, I integrated Cucumber BDD to facilitate collaboration between product and testing teams by expressing test scenarios in business-readable language.
I emphasized parallel execution capabilities by incorporating Selenium Grid 4 deployed via Docker containers, which improved test execution speed drastically and allowed for efficient resource management. The grid stability improved to 97% due to containerized architecture, ensuring minimal flakiness and downtime during parallel runs.
Furthermore, I embedded the automation suite into CI/CD pipelines using Jenkins and GitHub Actions, enforcing quality gates and enabling continuous testing with immediate feedback to development teams. This integration was pivotal in reducing regression cycles and catching defects early.
To complement the framework, I integrated logging, reporting, and failure analysis capabilities, which helped identify root causes rapidly and improve overall framework robustness. Our adoption of AI-assisted tools such as GitHub Copilot and Claude Code helped accelerate test script authoring and maintenance.
Finally, I ensured smooth adoption by coaching 8 Agile squads and mentoring 5+ QA engineers, standardizing best practices and reducing onboarding time by approximately 33%. This architected framework not only improved regression coverage and confidence but also delivered a 75% reduction in test execution time, demonstrating measurable business value.
